# MCU Hardware-Software Contract Audit

## Purpose

Prove the complete MCU contract from board net and physical package pin through alternate-function/remap, clock/power/reset, register or vendor SDK, ISR/DMA ownership, firmware build, programmed image, and observed board behavior. A matching symbol or successful compile does not prove the configured peripheral reaches the intended pin or runtime.

## Boundaries

- Fix MCU family/part/package, silicon revision, board/assembly revision, schematic revision, datasheet/manual/errata/difference-note revisions, SDK/tool package identity, compiler flags, and programmed firmware identity before comparing facts.
- Route document identity/integrity to `technical-document-intake`, electrical connectivity to `schematic-interface-audit`, vendor package/build/runtime identity to `multi-repo-artifact-provenance`, and generic software field crossings to `cross-layer-contract-audit`.
- Datasheet pin tables, user-manual register semantics, sibling-family difference notes, SDK headers, and example projects have different authority scopes. Similar names across MCU families do not prove register, AF, interrupt, DMA, or package compatibility.
- Read-only analysis is the default. Flashing, register writes, pin toggling, power changes, probing, continuity checks, or fault injection require explicit authorization and board-safe limits.

## Procedure

1. Freeze `mcu_scope`: exact part/family/package/revision, board variant, relevant rails/clocks, source hashes/revisions, SDK/toolchain identity, build target, binary hash, device identity, and verification time window.
2. Trace each board signal: source/destination refdes and net → MCU package pin → GPIO port/pin → AF/remap/mux selection → peripheral instance/channel. Preserve active-low, differential polarity, voltage domain, pulls, level shifting, DNP/zero-ohm options, and boot/debug multiplexing.
3. Trace enable conditions: supply domain, oscillator/PLL and bus clocks, reset source/release, peripheral clock gate, pin mode/speed/drive, AF register, peripheral enable, and required sequencing. Absence at any stage is a gap, not an implicit default.
4. Trace semantics from user manual/register definition → device header/SVD → vendor driver API → project wrapper/config → initialization call order → ISR/vector/NVIC → DMA request/channel/buffer ownership → consumer/task/state machine.
5. Compare family/revision differences and errata before borrowing code. Classify every reuse as `compatible`, `requires_adaptation`, `incompatible`, or `unresolved`; record field-level register/pin/clock/IRQ/DMA evidence.
6. Bind source package to build and programmed runtime. Prove which startup file, linker script, CMSIS/device header, vendor library, compile definitions, programmer target, binary, board instance, and runtime-loaded firmware identity were actually used.
7. Build a safe verification plan: static compile/link/map checks first, then authorized read-only register/log/logic evidence, and only then explicitly approved active measurement. Define expected observation, tolerance, rollback, stop condition, and unverified boundary.

## Required output contract

### `mcu_scope`

Exact MCU/part/package/silicon, board/assembly revision, document/SDK/toolchain/build/programmed-image identities, device/time boundary, and safety authorization.

### `pin_signal_matrix`

| Signal/net | Source/destination | Package pin | GPIO | AF/remap/mux | Peripheral/channel | Direction/polarity | Voltage/bias/protection | Population variant | Evidence | Status |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|

### `clock_power_reset_matrix`

| Peripheral/signal | Power domain | Clock source/tree/gate | Reset source/release | Init order/stable condition | Register/API evidence | Gap/risk | Status |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|

### `register_sdk_code_trace`

| Function | Manual register/field | Header/SVD symbol | SDK API | Project wrapper/config | Init order | Compile/link evidence | Status |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|

### `interrupt_dma_runtime_matrix`

| Peripheral event | IRQ/vector/NVIC | DMA request/channel | Buffer owner/lifetime | ISR/callback/task consumer | Runtime evidence | Status |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|

### `variant_compatibility`

Record source/target family, package, silicon and SDK versions; changed pin/register/clock/IRQ/DMA semantics; classification; required adaptation; evidence and unresolved errata.

### `verification_plan`

List static, build, programmed-image, read-only runtime, and explicitly authorized physical checks with expected result, tolerance, safety boundary, rollback/stop condition, evidence pointer, and status.

### `verdict`

State `proven`, `mismatch`, or `unresolved` per signal/peripheral and aggregate. Include the last proven boundary, allowed claim, prohibited overclaim, and smallest discriminating action.

## Anti-patterns

- Stopping at a schematic net label, pin macro, SDK call, successful build, programmer success, or log string.
- Copying an E50x/E51x or sibling-family example without field-level pin/register/clock/IRQ/DMA comparison.
- Verifying GPIO AF but not the clock gate, reset, vector table, DMA request, buffer lifetime, or programmed image.
- Treating a debugger/programmer package name as proof of supported target, host platform, license, or the actual flashed binary.
- Performing active board measurements or writes without explicit authorization and electrical safety limits.
